# /pr-self-check — Review the PR, Fix It, Push

The push-to-PR counterpart of `/self-check`. Same pipeline, different terminal
state: this one is allowed to push, and is therefore the skill where invariant 3
matters most.

Hold `.claude/skills/shared/safety-invariants.md` throughout.

## Step 1 — Fresh findings

Dispatch the `pr-reviewer` agent with the PR brief from the **Dispatch**
section of `.claude/skills/shared/review-pipeline.md` — verbatim, nothing
added. Under `/ship`, append `reuse-worktree <the run's worktree>` so the
review reads the branch exactly as the run left it.

Do not review the PR yourself, and do not add, drop or soften any finding it
returns: the agent's list *is* the finding set. Reviewing your own PR in the
context that wrote it is precisely the anchoring the agent exists to avoid.

The agent leaves the worktree in place and names it in its output — removal is
this skill's job, and only at the very end.

## Step 2 — Existing human comments

Fetch the PR's review comments. For each one written by a person (skip bots),
check it **against the current code** rather than against GitHub's resolved
flag: a thread marked resolved on a comment that was never actually addressed is
exactly the case worth catching. The code on the branch is the only gate.

This makes the finding set a **superset** of what `/review-pr` would report,
never a subset.

## Step 3 — Convert to fixes

Two passes, as in `/self-check`: enumerate everything first, then write fix
instructions. Tag each with an area and a fixability.

## Step 4 — Write the fix plan

`<% project.plans_dir %>/pr-<PR>-fixes.md`, in the main checkout — never in the
worktree, which is about to be removed. Resolve it with:

```bash
MAIN=$(dirname "$(git rev-parse --path-format=absolute --git-common-dir)")
```

Zero findings: say `No issues found.`, remove the worktree, stop.

## Step 5 — The gate

```
Reply: **all** · finding numbers (e.g. **1,3**) · **skip**
```

The report must be the **final message of the turn**.

## Step 6 — Fix, re-verify, re-review

Delegate by area. Then `.claude/scripts/verify.sh full`. Then dispatch a
**fresh** `pr-reviewer` with the same brief plus `reuse-worktree <path>` —
round 2 costs a fraction of round 1, which is what makes iterating to zero
findings affordable. Never continue the round-1 agent: it would re-review
anchored on its own earlier list.

Round counter in `selfCheck.round`, **written before the round runs**, capped
at 2.

## Step 7 — Push

Stage **by explicit named path**. Never `git add -A` without the artifact
excludes, and never the artifact dirs themselves.

```bash
git push origin HEAD:refs/heads/<headRefName>
```

The push guard blocks any HEAD whose tree `verify.sh` never passed (invariant
3). Commit everything the last `full` run verified — leave a file out and HEAD
is code that was never verified as a whole, so the push is refused.

Then, and only then, `git worktree remove --force <path>`. Pushing after the
removal pushes nothing.

## `--auto` mode

`/ship` passes `--auto`. It changes exactly two things: no approval gate, and
the outcome is decided by a table rather than by asking.

| Situation | `--auto` does |
|---|---|
| verdict is not `PASS` | **stop** — `push-blocked` stop record, worktree left in place |
| PR is no longer a draft | push anyway, and say so |
| head branch missing on the remote | **stop** — `push-blocked`; the PR stage never pushed |
| a fix touched a file outside the PR diff | push it, named and listed in the report |

On any stop, write the record first (`.claude/skills/shared/stop-record.md`) and
call `ScheduleWakeup({stop: true})`. Do **not** downgrade a blocked push into a
question: nobody is reading the terminal, so an unanswered question is just a
stalled run with its reason unrecorded.
